Subject: [PATCH]     USERSTORY Port Audit Log from MySQL
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

    Issue:
    ------
    MySQL on DStore don't support audit log currently which is the necessary feature
    for commercial use. We merge the MySQL's audit log function into MySQL on
    DStore.

    Solution:
    ------
    The audit log plugin will support DML、DCL、DDL and other commands like begin/commit/
    flush/set_option... Further more,connect and quit event will be recorded in the audit_log
    file in the default suitations.

    Variables:
    ------
    - include/exclude accounts: specify which user accounts should be included or excluded
    from audit logging.
    - include/exclude commands: specify which command types should be included or excluded
    from audit logging.
    - include/exclude database: specify which databases should be included or excluded from
    audit logging.
    - audit_log_format: The audit log plugin supports four log formats:CSV2, OLD, NEW, JSON,
    and CSV. OLD and NEW formats are based on XML.
    - audit_log_policy: This variable is used to specify which events should be logged. Possible
    values are:
      ALL - all events will be logged.
      LOGINS - only logins will be logged.
      QUERIES - only queries will be logged.
      NONE - no events will be logged.
    - audit_log_handler: This variable is used to configure where the audit log will be written.
    - audit_log_buffer_size: This variable can be used to specify the size of memory buffer used
    for logging.
    - audit_log_rotate_on_size: This variable is measured in bytes and specifies the maximum
    size of the audit log file. Upon reaching this size, the audit log will be rotated.
    - audit_log_rotations: This variable is used to specify how many log files should be kept
    when audit_log_rotate_on_size variable is set to non-zero value.
    - audit_log_strategy: This variable is used to specify the audit log strategy, possible
    values are:
      ASYNCHRONOUS - (default) log using memory buffer, do not drop messages if buffer is full.
      PERFORMANCE - log using memory buffer, drop messages if buffer is full.
      SEMISYNCHRONOUS - log directly to file, do not flush and sync every event.
      SYNCHRONOUS - log directly to file, flush and sync every event.
